The code statements that lead to the bug are:
kernel_fpu_begin() -> fpregs_lock() -> local_bh_disable()  
if (in_interrupt())  
    return;  
Reason:  
The change in kernel_fpu_begin() causes local_bh_disable() to set SOFTIRQ_OFFSET in the preempt count. This makes in_interrupt() return true during EFI runtime calls (which are in task context). The handler efi_crash_gracefully_on_page_fault() then always bails out via the "if (in_interrupt()) return;" line, leaving EFI firmware page faults unhandled and leading to a hard freeze.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!in_task())  
    return;  
Reason:  
Replacing in_interrupt() with !in_task() correctly distinguishes real interrupt/NMI context from task context. The !in_task() check returns true only when the code is running in hardirq, softirq, or NMI context, but not when only SOFTIRQ_OFFSET is set by local_bh_disable() in task context. This preserves the original intent of bailing out for genuine interrupts while allowing the graceful EFI page fault handler to work when the fault occurs during an EFI runtime service call.

